 ## Overview
-DINR is a sparse-view computed tomography (CT) reconstruction method that regularizes an Implicit Neural Representation (INR) using a pretrained diffusion model prior to suppress noise and artifacts in raw projection data [S1]. It addresses the ill-posed inverse problem of sparse-view CT, where insufficient projection views cause severe artifacts in traditional methods like Filtered Back Projection (FBP) [S1]. Unlike standard INRs, which suffer from spectral bias toward low-frequency components and instability under sparse supervision, DINR incorporates a proximal formulation that integrates denoised diffusion outputs into the INR loss [S1]. It differs from sibling methods like DD3IP by replacing the UNet-based volume representation with an INR, enabling resolution-independent and memory-efficient posterior mean estimation [S1].
+DINR is a sparse-view computed tomography (CT) reconstruction method that regularizes an Implicit Neural Representation (INR) using a pretrained diffusion model prior to suppress noise and artifacts in raw projection data [S1]. It addresses the ill-posed inverse problem of sparse-view CT, where insufficient projection views cause severe artifacts in traditional methods like Filtered Back Projection (FBP) [S1]. Unlike standard INRs, which suffer from spectral bias toward low-frequency components and instability under sparse supervision [S1], DINR incorporates a proximal formulation that integrates denoised diffusion outputs into the INR loss [S1]. It differs from sibling methods like DD3IP by replacing the UNet-based volume representation with an INR, enabling resolution-independent and memory-efficient posterior mean estimation [S1].
 
