 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Spectral Bias:** Standard INRs exhibit strong spectral bias toward low-frequency components, leading to poor reconstruction of high-frequency structure under sparse supervision. DINR mitigates this by incorporating the diffusion prior [S1].
-- **Initialization:** The initialization of $x_T$ uses the FBP estimate $A^* y$ corrupted with noise. The scaling parameter $\omega$ controls the regularization by scaling the values of $A^* y$ relative to the noise variance [S1].
-- **Parameter Tuning:** The parameter $\rho$ must be chosen carefully. In experiments, it was set such that the ratio of the proximal term to the data-fidelity term was $1 \times 10^{-6}$ or $1 \times 10^{-5}$ [S1]. The parameter $\omega$ was set to 0.002 [S1].
